    "q-146386": {
      "dom_id": "step-q146386",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=0.8$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=2.2,$ calculate the covariance between $X$ and $Y.$",
      "worked_solution": "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nWe use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(0)(1)(0.1)+(0)(2)(0.2)+(0)(3)(0.3) \\\\  & +(2)(1)(0.1)+(2)(2)(0.2)+(2)(3)(0.1) \\\\  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1.6-(0.8)(2.2) \\\\  & =-0.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
