    "q-147965": {
      "dom_id": "step-q147965",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.11$ | $0.44$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.09$ | $0.36$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$\n\n*Hint: It can be shown that $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible values of $x$ and $y.$*",
      "worked_solution": "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nWe're told that\n\n$$\nP(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values of $x$ and $y.$ Therefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent. \n\nSince $X$ are $Y$ are independent, we have $\\text{Cov}(X,Y)=0.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-147965-a",
